# UI Slice 9's verification: a virtualized list, actually scrolled.
#
# The claim is that `scroll_area_virtual` places only the rows its viewport
# covers *and* puts them exactly where a plain scroll area would. The first half
# is asserted in `slmsttaa-ui/tests/virtual_rows.rs`, where the recorder can see
# which rows were built. The second half is a picture, and these are the pictures:
# a list of six, a list of five thousand, and the same list part-way down.
#
# Run it:  cargo xtask shoot editor --script capture/editor-list.script
#
# Frame numbers, not seconds. The engine holds on each one, so a click lands on a
# known frame — which is what makes the coordinates below stable rather than a
# guess about timing.
#
# The panel coordinates are logical points anchored to the top-left corner, so
# they do not move with the window size. They *do* reflow: selecting an object
# grows the Selected section by eight rows and pushes everything under it down,
# which is why nothing here clicks a list row.

# Frame 120: six objects, the list not yet overflowing its own viewport.
120  shot  rest

# Five presses of +1000. Each is on its own frame so the click lands while the
# engine is parked, rather than somewhere in the gap between two.
140  move  78 390
140  click
160  click
180  click
200  click
220  click

# Frame 240: 5,006 rows. Six of them are on screen and six of them were built.
# `ui` in the HUD is the whole point of the slice — it read 11 ms here before.
240  shot  populated

# Into the list body — and then wait twenty frames before turning the wheel.
#
# That gap is load-bearing, and the first version of this script did not have it.
# A frozen frame runs `render` and nothing else, so the demo never *updates* with
# the pointer in its new place, and `ui_pointer` — which is deliberately one frame
# stale, see `EditorDemo::ui_pointer` — is still false when the notch arrives. The
# scroll goes to the camera instead of the list, and the picture is of a zoomed-in
# scene beside a list that did not move. Moving on an earlier frame lets a real
# update run in between.
260  move  120 300

# Two notches is 56 points, which is not a whole number of 22-point rows: the top
# row must straddle the viewport's edge rather than a blank strip opening above it.
280  wheel  -2
282  shot  midglide
320  shot  settled

# A long way down, where a row's index is nowhere near its position on screen.
340  wheel  -60
380  shot  scrolled

# And back to the top, which is the clamp that off-by-ones live in.
400  wheel  120
440  shot  backtotop
